P0011 Abstract
P0012 The regular dodecahedron is the forced carrier of the projection operator Π, established by the Fragility Topology (Theorems 1–16). The regular icosahedron is its dual action topology. The pentagonal faces of the dodecahedron are the semantic domains. This paper identifies a recursion: the pentagon assembles into the dodecahedron; the dodecahedron assembles into the 120-cell (the regular 4-polytope whose 120 cells are dodecahedra); the 120-cell admits no further regular convex extension. The recursion has depth 3 and terminates.
P0013 The dual recursion (triangle, icosahedron, 600-cell) terminates at the same depth. The governance margin μ, defined as dual-separation under shared symmetry, is shown to be non-compositional over cellular decomposition: 120-cell cells have μ > 0 and the whole has μ > 0; 600-cell cells have μ = 0 and the whole has μ > 0; 24-cell cells have μ > 0 and the whole has μ = 0. The 24-cell is the unique self-dual non-simplex regular convex 4-polytope and exists only at the terminal recursive depth.
P0014 The depth of the recursion (3) equals the cardinality of the TRIAD (□G, □S, □F). These derive independently: the recursion from the Coxeter classification of regular polytopes, the TRIAD from Axiom 1 (irreducibility of the three modalities of projection). The correspondence is established; a common-root theorem remains open.
P0015 Keywords: projection operator, dodecahedral recursion, 120-cell, 600-cell, 24-cell, governance margin, non-compositionality, TRIAD, depth-3 termination, H₄ symmetry
P0016 0. Primitives
P0017 D0.1 (Projection Operator). Π := the projection operator. Every persistent entity E is a section of Π: Π(E) = (□G(E), □S(E), □F(E)). The TRIAD is irreducible (Axiom 1).
P0018 D0.2 (Governance Margin). μ(P) = 0 ↔ D(P) ≅ P (self-dual). μ(P) > 0 ↔ D(P) ≇ P (non-self-dual). μ measures dual-separation: the capacity to assign maintenance and action to geometrically distinct carrier sets under shared symmetry. It does not measure size, curvature, group order, or graph distance.
P0019 D0.3 (Carrier Recursion). CARRIER(n) := the depth-n object in the pentagonal carrier recursion over regular convex polytopes, defined by CELL(CARRIER(n+1)) ≅ CARRIER(n). The recursion is defined only over regular convex polytopes satisfying this cell-identity condition.
P0020 D0.4 (Symmetry Groups). H₄ := Coxeter reflection group [3,3,5], |H₄| = 14400, symmetry group of 120-cell and 600-cell. S₅ := symmetry group of the 5-cell, |S₅| = 120. F₄ := Coxeter symmetry group of the 24-cell, |F₄| = 1152.
P0021 1. The Recursion
P0022 CARRIER(1) is the regular pentagon: 5 vertices, 5 edges, 1 face. CARRIER(2) is the regular dodecahedron: 20 vertices, 30 edges, 12 pentagonal faces. The 12 faces of the dodecahedron are 12 pentagons. CELL(CARRIER(2)) ≅ CARRIER(1).
P0023 CARRIER(3) is the regular 120-cell: 600 vertices, 1200 edges, 720 pentagonal 2-faces, 120 dodecahedral 3-cells. The 120 cells of the 120-cell are 120 dodecahedra. CELL(CARRIER(3)) ≅ CARRIER(2).
P0024 Theorem 1.1 (Recursion Termination). CARRIER(4) = ∅ under the pentagonal carrier recursion over regular convex polytopes. No regular convex 5-polytope has 120-cell cells.
P0025 Proof. The regular convex polytopes in dimension 5 and above comprise exactly three families: the n-simplex, the n-hypercube, and the n-cross-polytope (Coxeter classification). The cells of the 5-simplex are 5 tetrahedral 4-cells. The cells of the 5-hypercube are 10 tesseract 4-cells. The cells of the 5-cross-polytope are 32 pentachoron 4-cells. None has 120-cell cells. The recursion condition CELL(CARRIER(4)) ≅ CARRIER(3) = 120-cell is unsatisfiable. □
P0026 The recursion therefore has exactly three levels. CARRIER(1) is 2-dimensional, CARRIER(2) is 3-dimensional, CARRIER(3) is 4-dimensional, and no CARRIER(4) exists within the class of regular convex polytopes.
